//? Title: Gauss–Jordan elimination: actual inverse construction and its cost
//? Subtitle: Full augmented reduction, two-sided inverse diagnostics, actual work and honest cutoff failures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a square matrix and a right-hand side, compute both a solution of the
linear equations and a candidate matrix inverse. Row reduction must keep three
blocks consistent, while floating-point arithmetic can introduce error or reject
a pivot. Gauss–Jordan elimination carries an identity block through the same
operations as the equations. This report follows actual native snapshots on a
two-equation example, reruns the original three-equation system and checks both
inverse products without snapping small entries to zero. Measured work, a large
above-pivot clearing factor and cutoff/history controls expose the method's cost
and limits.
)

\toc

> Carry the identity block through a tiny solve

For x+y=3 and 2x+3y=8, swap row 1 above row 0 to select the larger first pivot.
Divide that row by 2, then clear the first column of the other row. Its second
pivot is -0.5. Normalizing it gives y=2; clearing above it gives x=1.
Every operation is also applied to an initially separate identity matrix.

The native trace records pivot after all row interchanges, normalize after the
whole pivot-row division, and eliminate after a complete other-row update.
It stores the actual evolving identity block, named transform, as well as the
coefficient matrix, RHS and row-interchange IDs. It does not replay the algorithm.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jordan_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
