\caption(The evolving identity block becomes the inverse when reduction succeeds in exact arithmetic. Snapshots keep the actual floating states; row IDs record swaps, not a permutation of solution coordinates.)
\alttext(Three native boxes show normalization by 2, below-pivot reduction to minus0.5y equals minus1, then normalization and clearing above to yield x1,y2. The evolving first identity row finishes at3,minus1.)

> Why carrying I constructs an inverse

A row interchange, multiplication of one row by a nonzero number and subtraction
of a multiple of another row are reversible. Let T be the product of these
row-operation matrices. In exact arithmetic they act on all three blocks:

$$
T[A\mid I\mid b]=[TA\mid T\mid Tb].
$$
\alttext(The accumulated row transformation acts equally on the coefficient matrix, identity block and right-hand side.)

If the coefficient block reduces to I, then TA=I. For a square matrix this makes
T the inverse, and Tb solves Ax=b. Thus the fully reduced augmented system is
[I | A^-1 | x]. For a failed pivot, T is only a partial row transformation; it
must not be labelled an inverse. Computed floating-point blocks need not be an
exact identity, even when the exact-arithmetic argument applies.

At column k the implementation selects the earliest largest absolute pivot among
remaining rows, swaps all three blocks, and applies

$$
\begin{aligned}
(A_{k,:},T_{k,:},b_k)&\leftarrow(A_{k,:},T_{k,:},b_k)/d,\qquad d=A_{kk},\\
(A_{r,:},T_{r,:},b_r)&\leftarrow(A_{r,:},T_{r,:},b_r)
 -f(A_{k,:},T_{k,:},b_k),\quad r\ne k,\quad f=A_{rk}.
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(Normalize the whole pivot row of all three blocks by its saved pivot, then subtract the current column entry times that normalized row from every other row.)

All columns of both matrix blocks are updated, including earlier columns and
zero clearing factors. No exact zero or identity is manufactured afterwards.
This is a square inverse-and-solve API, not a general rectangular rank or RREF
classifier. The related GJ presentation in
[Tsao (1989), NASA TM101466, Section3](https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19890011339)
reduces to diagonal form under a no-pivoting assumption. Our variant normalizes
pivot rows, uses partial pivoting and carries an explicit identity block.
Its operation ordering and storage are therefore stated directly rather than
borrowing another implementation's cost or a general error guarantee.

> The original three-equation system, with unsnapped products

Reuse 2x+y-z=8, -3x-y+2z=-11 and -2x+y+2z=-3. Their exact mathematical solution is
(2,3,-1); the exact inverse has rows (4,3,-1),(-2,-2,1),(5,4,-1).
Native fixtures and diagnostics replace the old report's local matrix-product
loop. Diagnostics recompute both A*V and V*A in checked long-double arithmetic,
where V is the supplied inverse candidate. They do not assume V is correct.
The figure shows the actual inverse and A*V-I, retaining small residual values.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jordan_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
