\image(figures/jordan_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original report displayed a product snapped to the identity below a threshold. This figure instead labels the actual widened residual, so small floating errors stay visible.)
\alttext(The computed inverse has rows approximately4,3,minus1; minus2,minus2,1; and5,4,minus1. A second grid shows scientific-notation values of A times V minus I without threshold snapping.)

For a product P, the diagnostic infinity error is max_i sum_j |P_ij-I_ij|.
The product of the supplied matrix norms is ||A||inf ||V||inf. It is a condition
number only when V is the true inverse; an arbitrary candidate's norm product
cannot certify conditioning. These computed residuals are not interval proofs
of inverse accuracy. Long double may have the same range and precision as double
on some platforms, so widening is checked rather than promised extra precision.

The same limitation applies to a solve residual: a small ||b-Ax|| alone needs a
conditioning argument before it bounds forward error. The
[LAPACK Users' Guide](https://netlib.org/lapack/lug/node81.html)
distinguishes normwise backward error from conditioning and forward error.
[G. Peters and J. H. Wilkinson (1975), On the Stability of Gauss-Jordan Elimination with Pivoting](https://doi.org/10.1145/360569.360653)
is the original focused stability reference; this report makes no unconditional
accuracy claim for the native implementation.

> The inverse block has an explicit cost

The old report described the inverse as free and quoted roughly 50% more work.
That description does not fit this full-block implementation. At each of n
columns it normalizes n entries in A, n entries in the identity block and one
RHS entry. It then clears n-1 other rows, updating all n entries of both blocks:

$$
\begin{aligned}
C_{A\rm\ updates}&=C_{T\rm\ updates}=n^2(n-1),\\
C_{A\rm\ divisions}&=C_{T\rm\ divisions}=n^2,\\
C_{\rm RHS\ updates}&=n(n-1),\qquad C_{\rm RHS\ divisions}=n.
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(Each matrix block receives n squared times n minus one multiply-subtract assignments and n squared normalization divisions; RHS updates are n times n minus one, with n divisions.)

One update means one multiply-subtract assignment, not one floating operation.
The native Gaussian solver's coefficient-update count is n(n-1)(n+1)/3. Comparing
that count with the two Gauss–Jordan matrix blocks gives a ratio 6n/(n+1) for n>1.
This compares these implementations performing different tasks: inverse-and-solve
versus solve-only. It is not a universal ratio, benchmark or timing claim.
Counts exclude validation, finite checks, allocation, diagnostics and trace copies.
Ordinary arithmetic is O(n³) and storage O(n²). Full histories retain O(n²)
events of O(n²) entries each, giving O(n⁴) copying and retained storage.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jordan_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gauss_jordan.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 std::vector<double>x,gaussian,jordan,history;
 std::print("| n | Gaussian coefficient updates | GJ coefficient updates | GJ identity-block updates | total GJ/Gaussian ratio | normalization divisions, both blocks | whole GJ trace entries |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(std::size_t n:std::array<std::size_t,7>{4,8,16,32,64,128,256}){
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::gaussian_elimination_growth_matrix(n);const std::vector<double>b(n,1);
  const auto g=dm::algorithms::gaussian_elimination_solve(a,b);const auto j=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_solve(a,b);const auto total=j.work.a_updates+j.work.transform_updates;const auto bound=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace_entries(n);
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {} | {} |\n",n,g.work.coefficient_updates,j.work.a_updates,j.work.transform_updates,double(total)/g.work.coefficient_updates,j.work.a_divisions+j.work.transform_divisions,bound);
  x.push_back(std::log2(double(n)));gaussian.push_back(std::log2(double(g.work.coefficient_updates)));jordan.push_back(std::log2(double(total)));history.push_back(std::log2(double(bound)));
  if(!g.solution.solved||!j.result.solved||j.work.a_updates!=n*n*(n-1)||j.work.transform_updates!=n*n*(n-1))throw std::runtime_error("full block work");
 }
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(x,gaussian,"Gaussian updates",{37,99,235},2).points(x,gaussian,"",{37,99,235},4)
  .lines(x,jordan,"GJ block updates",{220,38,38},2).points(x,jordan,"",{220,38,38},4)
  .lines(x,history,"History entries",{22,163,74},2).legend({"Gaussian updates","GJ block updates","History entries"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74}})
  .title("Counted arithmetic for different outputs, plus virtual history admission").xlab("log2 matrix order").ylab("log2 actual updates or virtual retained entries").y_limits(2,40).size(1100,540).save_svg("build/jordan_work.svg");
}
