\alttext(Two actual update-count curves grow cubically, with Gauss–Jordan carrying both full matrix blocks. A higher quartic curve shows virtual whole-history entries. The plot reports counts, not timings.)

> Clearing above a normalized pivot can use a large factor

Partial pivoting selects among the remaining rows in the current column.
The native elimination event records raw f=A[r][k] after the pivot row has been
normalized. This is not Gaussian elimination's ratio to an unnormalized pivot.
It need not be bounded by 1, and earlier rows above the pivot are not candidates
in that pivot search.

For A=[[1,100],[0,1]], the second pivot is 1 and its row needs no scaling change.
Clearing row 0 uses raw factor 100. This is an exact, hand-checkable illustration;
a large raw clearing factor alone is not a proof that the computed answer is bad.
The native inverse is [[1,-100],[0,1]], and both products are exactly I for these
representable inputs. Supplying I as a deliberately wrong inverse gives large
product errors, even though its supplied norm product is much smaller.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jordan_above.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
