> Cutoff failure leaves a partial transformation, not an inverse

The legacy absolute threshold remains 1e-12. Selected pivots strictly below it
return solved=false with empty solution and inverse arrays; equality is accepted.
This does not distinguish inconsistent from underdetermined systems and is not
a rank certificate. A uniformly scaled 1e-13 identity matrix is well conditioned
yet fails the default cutoff. New APIs expose an explicit positive finite cutoff;
choosing 1e-15 accepts that system without changing the legacy default.

All matrix and RHS shapes and finite entries are checked before pivoting,
including entries beyond an early zero pivot. Nonfinite stored arithmetic throws;
rounding, underflow and loss of accuracy can still occur. A thrown exception
returns no partial trace and does not mutate the caller.

A trace admits its whole conservative history before copies or inverse/state
allocation. With at most E=n²+n actual pivot/normalize/eliminate events:

$$
H(n)=n^2+2n+(n^2+n)(1+2n^2+4n).
$$
\alttext(History entries comprise the original matrix rows and cells plus RHS, then every possible event's metadata, two matrix blocks with row records, RHS and permutation.)

The count includes input and event matrix row records. It excludes ordinary final
output, root/work metadata, working state and allocator bytes: entries are not
bytes or peak RAM. Default cap is one million; zero disables only the optional
cap, while arithmetic, container and work-counter checks remain.
Successful two-row traces retain exactly the whole bound 110. A first-pivot
rejection retains only 25 entries, but still needs admission 110; cap 25 cannot
request an early-stop discount. There is no replay, truncation or partial output.
Inverse diagnostics use a separate product-row/cell quota with different units.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jordan_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gauss_jordan.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto singular=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace({{1,2},{2,4}},{1,3});
 const auto partial=dm::algorithms::inverse_residual_summary(singular.input,singular.events.back().transform);
 std::print("Singular/inconsistent example: solved {}; legacy solution size {}; legacy inverse rows {}; accepted pivots {}; final event {}; partial-transform inverse errors {:.1f}, {:.1f}.\n",singular.run.result.solved,singular.run.result.x.size(),singular.run.result.inverse.size(),singular.run.accepted_pivots,singular.events.back().kind,partial.left_error_inf,partial.right_error_inf);
 const dm::algorithms::GaussianMatrix tiny{{1e-13,0},{0,1e-13}};const std::vector<double>bsmall{1e-13,2e-13};const auto rejected=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_solve(tiny,bsmall);const auto accepted=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_solve(tiny,bsmall,1e-15);
 std::print("Scaled identity: default cutoff accepted {}; explicit 1e-15 accepted {}; solution {}.\n",rejected.result.solved,accepted.result.solved,accepted.result.x);
 dm::algorithms::GaussianMatrix zero{{0,0},{0,0}};std::vector<double>b{0,0};bool denied=false;try{(void)dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace(zero,b,25);}catch(const std::length_error&){denied=true;}
 auto early=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace(zero,b,110);zero.clear();b.clear();early.events[0].transform[0][0]=99;
 auto full=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace({{1,1},{2,3}},{3,8});full.events[0].transform[0][0]=99;
 const bool owned=early.input[0][0]==0&&early.run.reduced[0][0]==0&&full.events[1].transform[0][0]!=99&&full.run.result.inverse[0][0]!=99;
 bool shape=false,later_nan=false,arithmetic=false,virtual_count=false,product_quota=false;
 try{(void)dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace({{1,2},{3}},{1,2},1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){shape=true;}
 try{(void)dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace({{0,0},{0,std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN()}},{0,0},1);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){later_nan=true;}
 const double m=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();try{(void)dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan({{m,m},{-m,m}},{m,m});}catch(const std::overflow_error&){arithmetic=true;}
 try{(void)dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace_entries(std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max());}catch(const std::length_error&){virtual_count=true;}
 try{(void)dm::algorithms::inverse_residual_summary({{1,0},{0,1}},{{1,0},{0,1}},11);}catch(const std::length_error&){product_quota=true;}
 const auto empty=dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace({},{});
 std::print("History upper {}; successful actual {}; early-rejected actual {}; cap 25 rejected {}; cap 110 admitted true; independent owned copies {}.\n",dm::algorithms::gauss_jordan_trace_entries(2),full.captured_entries,early.captured_entries,denied,owned);
 std::print("Checks: shape before quota {}; later NaN before early pivot/quota {}; stored arithmetic overflow {}; virtual count overflow {}; independent product quota {}.\n",shape,later_nan,arithmetic,virtual_count,product_quota);
 std::print("Empty: solved {}; inverse rows {}; events {}; retained {}; inverse-product error {}.\n",empty.run.result.solved,empty.run.result.inverse.size(),empty.events.size(),empty.captured_entries,dm::algorithms::inverse_residual_summary({},{}).left_error_inf);
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({110.,double(full.captured_entries),double(early.captured_entries),25.},{"Whole admission","Successful history","Early rejection","Rejected cap"});plot.title("Successful histories attain the bound; early failure is not discounted").xlab("Two-row full augmented trace").ylab("Retained-history entries, not bytes").y_limits(0,130).size(1100,520).save_svg("build/jordan_controls.svg");
 if(!denied||!owned||!shape||!later_nan||!arithmetic||!virtual_count||!product_quota||rejected.result.solved||!accepted.result.solved)throw std::runtime_error("cutoff and resource controls");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Singular/inconsistent example: solved false; legacy solution size 0; legacy inverse rows 0; accepted pivots 1; final event reject; partial-transform inverse errors 6.0, 2.0.
Scaled identity: default cutoff accepted false; explicit 1e-15 accepted true; solution [1, 2].
History upper 110; successful actual 110; early-rejected actual 25; cap 25 rejected true; cap 110 admitted true; independent owned copies true.
Checks: shape before quota true; later NaN before early pivot/quota true; stored arithmetic overflow true; virtual count overflow true; independent product quota true.
Empty: solved true; inverse rows 0; events 0; retained 0; inverse-product error 0.
\image(figures/jordan_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Successful histories attain their bound because all event blocks have full size. A much shorter failed trace still needs conservative admission; its partial transformation is not returned as a legacy inverse.)
\alttext(Four bars show whole admission 110, successful history110, early rejection25 and a rejected cap 25. Computed controls cover partial inverse state, scaled cutoff, owned copies, full validation and empty input.)

The native trace makes inverse construction inspectable while keeping the exact
operation ordering of the original implementation. It also makes the costs and
failure semantics visible. Inverse products, solve residuals, partial-pivot
choices and resource admission answer separate questions; none supplies an
unconditional certificate of accuracy. These exact generated systems need no
external dataset and do not make an elapsed-time claim.
